│ patch_type               │ diff                                                                                      │
│ patch_name               │ trend_flat_hysteresis                                                                     │
│ patch_description        │ Introduce a persistent two-state trend/flat controller driven by the local regression     │
│                          │ slope relative to its estimated slope noise. Entering trend mode requires stronger        │
│                          │ evidence over consecutive windows, while returning to flat mode uses a lower threshold    │
│                          │ and longer confirmation. This prevents gain and reversal logic from reacting to noisy     │
│                          │ threshold crossings.                                                                      │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ In flat mode, velocity adaptation is deliberately conservative and output reversals       │
│                          │ require three confirmations with a wider deadband. In trend mode, velocity adapts faster  │
│                          │ and genuine turns retain the existing two-sample confirmation. This targets the high      │
│                          │ false-reversal and slope-change counts without applying a uniform slower filter that      │
│                          │ would worsen lag during sustained signal motion.                                          │
