│ patch_name               │ hysteretic_multiscale_tracker                                                             │
│ patch_description        │ The current approach uses a Kalman level/slope tracker with a short two-sample reversal   │
│                          │ confirmation. Its primary weakness is excessive directional switching: slope changes      │
│                          │ increased to 189.4 and false reversals to 151 despite good lag (0.31) and accuracy        │
│                          │ (0.91).                                                                                   │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ This revision replaces covariance-heavy updates with a robust multi-scale                 │
│                          │ endpoint-regression alpha-beta tracker. Key changes are: (1) a recency-weighted robust    │
│                          │ regression observation rather than direct noisy measurement updates, (2) short- and       │
│                          │ long-window slope consensus for trend evidence, (3) a two-state flat/trend controller     │
│                          │ with hysteresis thresholds of 1.35 entry, 0.70 exit, 2 entry windows, and 3 exit windows, │
│                          │ (4) lower alpha/beta gains in flat conditions (`0.24`, `0.035`) and higher gains in       │
│                          │ sustained trends (`0.52`, `0.15`), and (5) three-confirmation reversals in flat state     │
│                          │ versus two in trend state. This should reduce noise-driven reversals and slope changes    │
│                          │ while retaining rapid tracking when repeated slope evidence indicates a genuine move.     │
│                          │ Robust Huber weighting and innovation clipping reduce impulse sensitivity without adding  │
│                          │ non-causal delay.                                                                         │
