│ patch_type               │ full                                                                                      │
│ patch_name               │ robust_multiscale_ratchet                                                                 │
│ patch_description        │ This design replaces the prior Kalman-consensus architecture with a causal multiscale     │
│                          │ robust-regression filter and a separate directional ratchet controller.                   │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ The filter estimates endpoint level and slope from two exponentially weighted local       │
│                          │ polynomial regressions: a short horizon for rapid dynamics and a long horizon for noise   │
│                          │ suppression. A Huber-style residual clipping pass prevents isolated spikes from           │
│                          │ distorting either fit. Their blend is selected from slope confidence and cross-scale      │
│                          │ agreement, rather than continuously varying Kalman covariance gains.                      │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ A directional ratchet then treats visible reversals as explicit events: movement in the   │
│                          │ current direction is passed with predictive smoothing, while countertrend movement must   │
│                          │ have slope support and repeated evidence unless it is a large, noise-normalized turn.     │
│                          │ This targets the prior programs' main weakness—high false reversal and slope-change       │
│                          │ counts—without introducing the substantial delay of a moving average.                     │
