│ patch_type               │ full                                                                                      │
│ patch_name               │ robust_trendgate                                                                          │
│ patch_description        │ The previous approach used a three-scale regression bank, adaptive constant-velocity      │
│                          │ Kalman tracker, innovation clipping, consensus blending, and a two-sample reversal gate.  │
│                          │ Its principal weakness was that trend confidence compared a per-sample slope directly     │
│                          │ against a window-scale noise value, causing coherent ramps to be classified too often as  │
│                          │ noise. It also used residual scale without centering residuals in the current version.    │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ This version retains the low-latency causal architecture but changes the configuration as │
│                          │ follows:                                                                                  │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ - Uses robust window MAD only for measurement clipping, with a 3.0 MAD clipping limit.    │
│                          │ - Fits two clipped causal endpoint regressions with decay strengths 1.6 and 4.2.          │
│                          │ - Computes centered post-fit residual MAD from the medium regression for trend            │
│                          │ confidence, reversal thresholds, and local-reference reliability.                         │
│                          │ - Measures slope evidence over the effective window span: `abs(slope) * (window_size - 1) │
│                          │ / residual_noise`, allowing clean ramps to be recognized as coherent trends.              │
│                          │ - Uses agreement between gentle and recent regression slopes before enabling predictive   │
│                          │ endpoint weighting.                                                                       │
│                          │ - Increases reversal confirmation from two samples to three samples unless the turn has   │
│                          │ strong endpoint and velocity support.                                                     │
│                          │ - Uses a stronger volatility-scaled reversal threshold and an adaptive deadband to reduce │
│                          │ false reversals and slope changes.                                                        │
│                          │ - Keeps a constant-velocity Kalman state and predictive consensus stage for               │
│                          │ responsiveness and low phase delay.                                                       │
